  message: "GRANTS LANDED — 20 of 33 audited modules, rust's first. This item's statement\nis that rust.yaml declares ZERO module_completeness entries so no rust module\ncan ever be an examined negative. The catalogue half of that is now false by\nconstruction; the BEHAVIOURAL half is measured below and is more interesting\nthan a pass/fail.\n\nHOW THE 20 WERE REACHED. The probe left 18 candidates over 33 modules and never\ngrants, so each was adjudicated against the source. WI-dukom had already done\nfour (SocketAddr, ErrorKind, ptr, slice); WI-pavob had settled three\n(std::sync and std::sync::Mutex declarable — a futex moves no data;\nstd::sync::mpsc's grant stands — a channel is intra-process). The remaining 13\nwere adjudicated here with a ONE-HOP reach checker, because a module's own text\nbeing clean is not the question the audit asks.\n\nTHE ONE THAT NEARLY WENT WRONG, and the reason a one-hop check was built:\n\n  std::collections::HashMap — NO SIGNAL from the probe. But HashMap::new()\n  returns HashMap<K, V, RandomState>, and RandomState's initialiser calls\n  crate::sys::random::hashmap_random_keys — an OS entropy read. Invisible to a\n  text scan of map.rs because RandomState is DEFINED IN ANOTHER MODULE.\n  A DEFAULT TYPE PARAMETER CAN CARRY A BOUNDARY. Same shape as Deref, and\n  exactly the re-export class the probe's own docstring predicted it could not\n  see.\n\nIt kept the grant on CROSS-LANGUAGE CONSISTENCY rather than judgement: the\nshipped python catalogue grants secrets, random AND uuid — all three draw on\nthe OS RNG — on the recorded reasoning that OS-RNG seeding belongs to the row\nof the module that PERFORMS it (os.urandom), not the surface of the module that\nconsumes it; and os.getrandom was removed from env_read outright with \"a CSPRNG\nread is not an environment read under any reading\". This vocabulary has NO KIND\nFOR ENTROPY. Refusing rust's hash collections on a read python grants would\nmake module_completeness mean different things per language, which is the\nfailure mode the axis discipline exists to prevent. A test pins the dependency\nso the rationale cannot silently outlive its basis.\n\nTWO MORE PROBE BUGS, bringing the campaign total to seven, none of which ever\nreported an error:\n  6. A MODULE CAN BE A FILE AND A DIRECTORY AT ONCE. std/src/fs.rs is the\n     module; std/src/fs/ beside it holds only tests.rs, and is_dir() fired\n     first — so std::fs and std::process were each adjudicated AGAINST THEIR\n     OWN TEST FILE. Both still refused (the tests name File and Command\n     because they exercise them), so it cost nothing here; a module with thin\n     tests would have read as having no surface at all.\n  7. MINE, in the one-hop checker: `crate::` was resolved to std regardless of\n     the importing file's crate, so core-resident std::mem and std::iter were\n     checked against std's tree and flagged for an entropy read and a\n     gethostname NEITHER CAN REACH.\n\nWHAT IS DELIBERATELY REFUSED — 13 modules, `std` foremost, because granting the\nwhole standard library turns every unmatched std call into an examined\nnegative. The clock modules are refused for the OPPOSITE reason to the rest:\nWI-pavob ruled a clock read IS host_info_read, so std::time and\nstd::time::SystemTime need ROWS (WI-tubij), not a no-I/O declaration.\n\nArtifacts: docs/surveys/rust-stdlib-module-io-enumeration.md (33-module verdict\ntable, all seven probe bugs, and the refusals with reasons);\npackages/hypergumbo-core/tests/test_rust_module_completeness.py;\n~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/lutuh_grants_08272026/ and lutuh_grants2_08272026/\n(the A/B, both arms, with compare.py).\n\nTHE MEASUREMENT, and it is the honest half of this closure.\n\nA/B over 13 rust repositories, both arms, fresh XDG_CACHE_HOME per arm, arms\nstrictly sequential because the catalogue path is fixed relative to the\ninstalled module. Arm-took proven each time: the old arm reports 0\nmodule_completeness entries, the new arm 20. arti timed out at 900s in BOTH\narms and is excluded from the denominator. Artifacts:\n~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/lutuh_grants_08272026/ and lutuh_grants2_08272026/.\n\n  36 claims over 12 repos.  0 VERDICTS MOVED.\n\nBlocker census for the 14 that are still inconclusive:\n  a language with NO I/O catalogue at all ............ 8\n  method-starvation ................................. 4\n  module_completeness -- the rung these grants feed .. 2\n\nONLY 2 OF 14 REACH THIS ITEM'S RUNG, AND BOTH IMPROVED: rage's unadjudicable\nmodule count falls 33 -> 25 on both of its inconclusive claims. The residual is\nthird-party crates (age::scrypt, age_core::*), which are out of catalogue scope\nunder the standing stdlib-scoped ruling, so no quantity of stdlib grants can\nclear it.\n\nWHY A VERDICT-ONLY A/B WOULD HAVE BEEN A FALSE NEGATIVE ABOUT THIS WORK. \"0\nmoved\" reads as \"the grants do nothing\". The count and the census say \"the\ngrants fire, and four other rungs stand in front of them\" -- and those two\nreadings call for opposite next actions. That is why the instrument records\nthe unclassified-module count and classifies the blocker, not just the verdict.\nIt is also why a GATE-LEVEL POSITIVE CONTROL ships in the test file: a call\ninto a granted module now yields coverage.complete True, with an ungranted\nstdlib module, a third-party crate, and an undeclared sibling all still\nwithholding in the same run. No cohort measurement can make that distinction.\n\nWHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT CLOSE. The statement has three clauses:\n  \"rust.yaml declares zero module_completeness entries\"  -- FALSE now, 20.\n  \"no Rust module can ever be an examined negative\"      -- FALSE now, proven\n      at the gate and by rage's count.\n  \"every clean Rust boundary verdict is withheld\"        -- STILL TRUE, and NOT\n      for this item's reason. Filed separately: a Cargo `[[bin]]` target\n      declaration is typed as a `calls` edge whose language slot is `toml`, and\n      there is no toml catalogue, so `languages_with_calls - set(catalogs)`\n      withholds every claim on every rust binary crate no matter how complete\n      the rust catalogue becomes.\n\nSo this closes the cause it names and hands the symptom to the cause that\nactually dominates it. Closing `done` rather than leaving it open on a clause\nit was never the cause of."  # a483
